proofs.proof_tex:
The upper bound follows immediately from \(\mathrm{thm:total\mbox{-}upper}\), because \(\widehat\tau_n(0)\) is one of the Borel estimators over which the infimum in \(\mathrm{def:minimax\mbox{-}risk}\) is taken:
\[
R_n\le
\sup_{P\in\mathcal P(d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)}
\mathbb E_{P_O^{\otimes n}}|\widehat\tau_n(0)-\tau_P(0)|
\le C\rho_n. \tag{42}
\]

For the converse, let \(\Pi_+,\Pi_-\) be the finite priors supplied by \(\mathrm{thm:fixed\mbox{-}n\mbox{-}component\mbox{-}converse}\), let \(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-\) be their actual fixed-\(n\) observed-data product mixtures, and denote their constant target values by \(\theta_+,\theta_-\).  Put \(\Delta=|\theta_+-\theta_-|\ge c_0\rho_n\).  From the elementary Cauchy--Schwarz calculation
\[
\begin{split}
\|\mathsf M_+-\mathsf M_-\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
&=\frac12\int|\sqrt{m_+}-\sqrt{m_-}|(\sqrt{m_+}+\sqrt{m_-})\\
&\le\frac12H(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-)
\left\{\int(\sqrt{m_+}+\sqrt{m_-})^2\right\}^{1/2}
\le H(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-),
\end{split}
\]
the squared Hellinger bound \(1/4\) implies total variation at most \(1/2\).

Fix an arbitrary Borel estimator \(T\), and test the two mixture hypotheses by choosing the target value closer to \(T\), with deterministic tie breaking.  On a testing error, \(|T-\theta_\varepsilon|\ge\Delta/2\).  If \(\varphi\in\{+,-\}\) denotes this test, then
\[
\begin{split}
\sup_{P\in\mathcal P}\mathbb E_P|T-\tau_P(0)|
&\ge\frac12\sum_{\varepsilon\in\{+,-\}}
\int\mathbb E_P|T-\theta_\varepsilon|\,d\Pi_\varepsilon(P)\\
&\ge\frac\Delta4\{\mathsf M_+(\varphi=-)+\mathsf M_-(\varphi=+)\}\\
&\ge\frac\Delta4\{1-\|\mathsf M_+-\mathsf M_-\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\}
\ge\frac{c_0}{8}\rho_n. 
\end{split} \tag{43}
\]
The penultimate inequality follows directly by writing the two test errors over the acceptance set and its complement and using the variational definition of total variation.  Taking the infimum over every Borel \(T\) in (43) proves \(R_n\ge c\rho_n\).  Both priors are supported on the same exact-uniform, bounded-overlap Bernoulli class and their mixtures are sums of genuine \(P_O^{\otimes n}\) laws, so (43) neither enlarges the model nor changes the experiment.  Combining (42)--(43) proves the claimed sharp pure-power rate and attainment by the total estimator.
